@page "/components/ai/imagepartview"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/imagepartview"
            Title="AiImagePartView"
            Description="BitAiImagePartView shows an image part of an AI message - one a model generated or a tool returned - lazily loaded, at its intrinsic size, with its caption." />

<DemoPage Name="AiImagePartView"
          Description="BitAiImagePartView shows an image part of a message - one a model generated or a tool returned - in a figure with its caption. It declares the intrinsic size of the image where the part knows it, so the message list does not jump as the image decodes, and loads it lazily, so a long transcript only fetches the images that scroll into view. The alternative text of the part is the accessible name of the image. Like the images of Markdown, it follows the image policy of the options: an image the policy holds back is a button that loads it, and one it blocks is its alternative text."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          GitHubAIUrl="ImagePartView/BitAiImagePartView.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/ImagePartView/BitAiImagePartViewDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            A chat renders it for every <b>BitAiImagePart</b> of a message (through BitAiMessagePartView); render it yourself to
            show an image part anywhere else. It needs no chat session, only the part. A model will not always describe the
            image it returns: ask for an alternative text in the prompt or supply one, since without it the image is marked
            decorative and a screen reader skips it.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The image follows the image policy of the options (<b>BitAiUiOptions.Images</b>, a
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview#image-policy">BitAiImagePolicy</BitLink>), as the images written inside
            Markdown do: one the policy loads only on request is a placeholder that loads it on click, and one it blocks is replaced
            by its alternative text. A relative URL is resolved against the base address of the document before the policy is
            asked, so listing the app's own host with <b>AllowListedHosts</b> shows its images; a URL of a scheme that can run
            script is never loaded, whatever the policy.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                <b>Part</b> is the <b>BitAiImagePart</b> to show: its <b>Url</b> (absolute, relative or a data URL), its <b>Alt</b>
                text and, where known, its intrinsic <b>Width</b> and <b>Height</b>, which reserve the room the image takes before it
                has loaded. It is never wider than its container, and its height follows its width.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiImagePartView Part="auroraImage" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Caption" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                A <b>Caption</b> of the part is rendered under the image as the caption of its figure.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiImagePartView Part="mountainImage" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Decorative image" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A part without an <b>Alt</b> renders an empty alternative text, which marks the image decorative: assistive
                technologies skip it. Without a <b>Width</b> and a <b>Height</b> the room for the image is only known once it has
                loaded.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiImagePartView Part="decorativeImage" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Data URL" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                A generated image often arrives inline, as a <b>data:</b> URL - an image a tool drew or a model returned as base64 -
                and is shown like any other, with no request made for it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiImagePartView Part="chartImage" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Image policy" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                The image follows <b>BitAiUiOptions.Images</b>, the
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview#image-policy">BitAiImagePolicy</BitLink> the images of Markdown follow
                too, cascaded here by a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/optionsprovider">BitAiOptionsProvider</BitLink>. Under
                <b>ClickToLoad</b> (the first) nothing is fetched until the reader presses the placeholder, a button named after the
                <b>Alt</b> of the part; under <b>Block</b> (the second) the alternative text stands in for the image. The caption is
                shown either way. A relative URL, like this one, is resolved against the base address of the page before the policy
                is asked, so <b>AllowListedHosts</b> with the app's own host shows it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="clickToLoadOptions">
                <BitAiImagePartView Part="policyImage" />
            </BitAiOptionsProvider>
            <br />
            <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="blockOptions">
                <BitAiImagePartView Part="policyImage" />
            </BitAiOptionsProvider>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root figure, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> its <b>Image</b> and its
                <b>Caption</b> - and <b>ImagePlaceholder</b> and <b>BlockedImage</b>, what stands in for an image the policy holds
                back. The public <b>--bit-AiImagePartView-*</b> variables (see the CSS variables table) restyle every image part view
                under the element they are set on.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
                <BitAiImagePartView Part="styledImage" Style="max-width: 18rem; padding: 0.5rem; border: 1px dashed dodgerblue;" />

                <BitAiImagePartView Part="styledImage" Class="custom-class" />

                <BitAiImagePartView Part="styledImage"
                                    Styles="@(new() { Root = "max-width: 18rem;",
                                                      Image = "filter: grayscale(1);",
                                                      Caption = "text-align: center; font-weight: bold;" })" />

                <BitAiImagePartView Part="styledImage"
                                    Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-root", Image = "custom-image", Caption = "custom-caption" })" />

                <div style="max-width: 18rem; --bit-AiImagePartView-radius: 2rem; --bit-AiImagePartView-caption-color: mediumseagreen; --bit-AiImagePartView-caption-font-size: 1rem;">
                    <BitAiImagePartView Part="styledImage" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the caption is set from the right, whether the direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a
                <b>dir</b> attribute around the figure.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiImagePartView Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Part="rtlImage" />
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
